585	/// Editing INSIDE a nest over MCP, and looking inside one: the envelope's
586	/// `scope` (spec §6, `core/src/scope.rs`) and the `set_scope` tool that
587	/// mirrors the double-click (road map 2's phase 9).
588	///
589	/// **The whole surface is arguments on tools that already existed.** No op
590	/// was added — `rc_op_names` was unchanged — so the catalog grew one property,
591	/// injected once in `AgentCatalog.tool` for every name the core calls an op,
592	/// and one tool, which is not an op either. What that buys is the thing the
593	/// spec designed and left (§4.4): every row of §6 can now name a clip, a
594	/// track or an action inside a nest.
595	///
596	/// Three pieces, and the split between them is the point:
597	///
598	///  - **The scope is read ONCE, at the dispatch.** `AgentServer.dispatch` is
599	///    the only place holding both a tool's name and its arguments for all
600	///    seventy-five of them, so it reads `scope` there and leaves it on
601	///    `callScope` for the length of the call; `performGroupedEdit` stamps it
602	///    onto every op through `addressed` below. Fifty-eight handlers were not
603	///    touched and cannot forget.
604	///  - **The id readers resolve against a CONTAINER.** `idScope` below is what
605	///    `clipArg`, `clipsArg`, `optionalClipsArg` and `trackArg` ask, so
606	///    `trim_start {clip: <inside the nest>, scope: 4}` finds its clip instead
607	///    of being refused by the app before the core ever saw the scope. That
608	///    was the real work of this phase on this side, and the road map does not
609	///    mention it.
610	///  - **`set_scope` is view state and edits nothing**, exactly like
611	///    `set_playhead`, `set_range` and `set_selection`. It exists because
612	///    `get_project` and `render_frame` answer about what the app is LOOKING
613	///    at, and without it there was no way to make them look inside. An op
614	///    sent while it is open follows it unless it carries a `scope` of its
615	///    own — `effectiveScope` below is that rule and argues it.
616	///
617	/// **Two composites take no scope ARGUMENT and still work inside a nest**,
618	/// and the ops-only injection rule excludes them without an exception list:
619	/// `detect_silence` and `normalize_clip` are not §6 rows. They follow the
620	/// VIEW instead, from both ends at once — their ids resolve through
621	/// `idScope` like every other tool's, and `SilenceDetector.read` /
622	/// `AudioNormalize.read` compute from `ProjectDocument.editedProject`'s plan,
623	/// which is the same container — so the pair is coherent rather than half
624	/// converted. What they cannot do is be pointed at a nest nobody is looking
625	/// at, and that is the argument for leaving it there: each one measures a
626	/// PLAN, the app memoises exactly one scoped handle (the open one), and a
627	/// per-call `scope` would mean building a second project per call and
628	/// mapping every tick in the answer back out of it. Nothing is lost that a
629	/// caller cannot reach — `set_scope`, then ask — and `ripple_delete_ranges`,
630	/// which is what `detect_silence`'s answer is fed to, takes a scope like any
631	/// other op.
632	///
633	/// There is also a spelling hazard here worth naming, since it is the reason
634	/// to think twice before changing that: `detect_silence` already answers a
635	/// RESULT key called `scope`, meaning which set of CLIPS it measured.
636	/// Nothing keeps those two words apart but the fact that one is an input and
637	/// the other an answer, so a `detect_silence` that ever took the envelope's
638	/// scope would have to say in both descriptions which is which.
639	///
640	/// **The captions group used to be the one place this did NOT line up, and
641	/// the way it was settled is the rule for the next producer.** Every one of
642	/// them — `CaptionImporter.plan(for:in:)`, `Transcriber`,
643	/// `TranscribeSheetController` and `CaptionExporter`, on the File-menu path
644	/// and the tool path alike — built its plan from `document.project`, the
645	/// DOCUMENT, while the ops that plan produced were addressed to whatever nest
646	/// was open, here and in `ProjectDocument.addressed(_:)` alike. So with a nest
647	/// open they measured a placement against the timeline and then placed it
648	/// inside the nest, and the menu item had exactly the same defect as the tool.
649	/// All four read `editedProject` now, as `SilenceDetector` and
650	/// `AudioNormalize` do. **A producer reads the container it writes into** —
651	/// that is the whole rule, it is cheaper than making the ops document-level,
652	/// and it is what a fifth producer should copy.
653	extension AgentServer {
654	
655	    // MARK: - The call's scope
656	
657	    /// Refuses a `scope` that names no nest in `document`, by name.
658	    ///
659	    /// Called from `target(_:)`, which every handler passes through before it
660	    /// reads a single argument, so the ORDER `core/src/scope.rs` documents
661	    /// holds on this side too: an unknown nest is answered FIRST, ahead of
662	    /// the op's own arguments, because it is the argument that decides which
663	    /// container the caller thinks it is editing.
664	    ///
665	    /// The sentence is the CORE's, word for word, with the app's own
666	    /// direction added after it. Both layers can answer this — the app for
667	    /// every op tool, the core for anything that reaches it another way — and
668	    /// a caller (or a proof) that learns one spelling should not have to
669	    /// learn a second.
670	    func checkCallScope(_ document: ProjectDocument) throws {
671	        guard let scope = callScope else { return }
672	        guard document.project.snapshot.nest(scope) != nil else {
673	            throw ToolError(
674	                message: "no nest with id \(scope). get_project lists them under nests, and a "
675	                    + "nest clip's source.project names the one it shows.")
676	        }
677	    }
678	
679	    /// The container this call's id arguments resolve in: the timeline, or
680	    /// the nest the envelope's `scope` named.
681	    ///
682	    /// It reads the PARENT snapshot rather than building a scoped handle,
683	    /// which is the cheap half of `core/src/scope.rs`'s view and the only
684	    /// half an id lookup needs: a nest holds its tracks and clips in the
685	    /// parent's one id space (spec §5.2), so `snapshot.nests[]` already has
686	    /// them. `NestScope` next door reads the path the same way and for the
687	    /// same reason.
688	    /// The nest a call is really acting in: its own `scope` when it has one,
689	    /// else the nest the APP has open (`set_scope`), else nil.
690	    ///
691	    /// **The argument overrides the view; an absent argument follows it.**
692	    /// That is the whole rule, and it is the one that makes every other piece
693	    /// agree with itself. `get_project` reports the open container, so the
694	    /// ids a caller reads back are that container's; `render_frame` draws it;
695	    /// `detect_silence`, `normalize_clip` and `sync_audio` compute from
696	    /// `ProjectDocument.editedProject`'s plan, which is that container's too;
697	    /// and `ProjectDocument.addressed(_:)` already stamps the open nest onto
698	    /// every op the APP sends, so an agent that inherits it is doing exactly
699	    /// what the person at the keyboard does. The alternative — the argument
700	    /// or nothing — was written first and thrown away: it made
701	    /// `get_project` list clips that the very next op could not name, and it
702	    /// would have left the three composites resolving their ids in one
703	    /// container and reading their plan from another.
704	    ///
705	    /// What it costs is the spelling of "edit the timeline while I am looking
706	    /// inside a nest", which is now `set_scope` with no arguments first. That
707	    /// is the same thing the UI costs a person, and it is worth less than the
708	    /// coherence.
709	    func effectiveScope(_ document: ProjectDocument) -> UInt32? {
710	        callScope ?? document.scope
711	    }
712	
713	    /// The container this call's id arguments resolve in — `effectiveScope`'s
714	    /// nest, or the timeline.
715	    ///
716	    /// It reads the PARENT snapshot rather than building a scoped handle,
717	    /// which is the cheap half of `core/src/scope.rs`'s view and the only
718	    /// half an id lookup needs: a nest holds its tracks and clips in the
719	    /// parent's one id space (spec §5.2), so `snapshot.nests[]` already has
720	    /// them. `NestScope` next door reads the path the same way and for the
721	    /// same reason.
722	    func idScope(_ document: ProjectDocument) throws -> AgentIdScope {
723	        let snapshot = document.project.snapshot
724	        guard let scope = effectiveScope(document) else {
725	            return AgentIdScope(
726	                nest: nil, clips: snapshot.clips, tracks: snapshot.tracks, fromArgument: false)
727	        }
728	        // Unreachable through `target(_:)`, which has already refused an
729	        // unknown `scope` argument, and through `clampViewState`, which
730	        // clears a view path whose nest has gone. It is here so the function
731	        // is total rather than optional-returning: a reader that fell back to
732	        // the timeline when the nest was missing would answer "No clip has id
733	        // 17" for a call whose real mistake was the scope.
734	        guard let nest = snapshot.nest(scope) else {
735	            try checkCallScope(document)
736	            return AgentIdScope(
737	                nest: nil, clips: snapshot.clips, tracks: snapshot.tracks, fromArgument: false)
738	        }
739	        return AgentIdScope(
740	            nest: nest, clips: nest.clips, tracks: nest.tracks, fromArgument: callScope != nil)
741	    }
742	
743	    /// The scope stamped onto the ops of one agent edit — `performGroupedEdit`
744	    /// in the frozen file calls this, and it is the MCP mirror of
745	    /// `ProjectDocument.addressed(_:)`.
746	    ///
747	    /// **Two rules, and the difference between them is deliberate.** An
748	    /// EXPLICIT `scope` is stamped on every op, the seven document-level ones
749	    /// included, so an agent that asks for `add_media {scope: 4}` gets the
750	    /// core's own refusal by name rather than a silent redirection — it asked
751	    /// a question and the answer is no (`ReelOp.documentLevelNames` argues
752	    /// the other half of this). An INHERITED one skips them, exactly as
753	    /// `addressed` does, because a Finder drop inside a nest is an
