852	    /// Where an edit's ops actually LANDED, as the result reports it: the nest
853	    /// whose id was stamped on them, and the names of any that went to the
854	    /// document instead.
855	    ///
856	    /// **It is read off what `addressed` STAMPS and never off
857	    /// `effectiveScope`**, and that difference is the whole reason it exists.
858	    /// `editResult` used to echo the effective scope unconditionally, which is
859	    /// a statement about the VIEW; `addressed` sends the seven document-level
860	    /// ops unscoped out of an inherited one, which is a statement about the
861	    /// EDIT. So a `remove_media`, a `set_canvas` or a marker rename sent with
862	    /// a nest open reported that it had landed inside that nest when it had
863	    /// landed on the document — a tool misreporting WHERE an edit went, which
864	    /// is the class of defect this phase already fixed once at the merge seam
865	    /// (`performGroupedEdit`'s closing guard).
866	    ///
867	    /// **The MIXED edit is why `document_ops` is a list and not a flag.** One
868	    /// edit can legitimately span both containers — a Finder drop inside a
869	    /// nest is `add_media` on the document plus `add_clip` in the nest, as ONE
870	    /// undo step (`ReelOp.documentLevelNames` argues it) — and there is no
871	    /// single number that describes that, which is exactly the mistake being
872	    /// fixed. So both halves are named: `scope` is the nest the scoped ops
873	    /// carried and `document_ops` is every op that skipped it, and a result
874	    /// carrying both says which of its ops went where. No tool sends such a
875	    /// mix TODAY — an agent's `import_media` and `add_clip` are two calls, and
876	    /// `add_background`'s Image… composite is the menu's rather than the
877	    /// tool's — but the account has to be right before the composite that does
878	    /// arrives, and nobody reading one of these results should have to know
879	    /// which handlers mix and which do not.
880	    ///
881	    /// With no scope anywhere it answers nothing at all, so every ordinary
882	    /// result is byte for byte the one it always was.
883	    func scopeLanding(_ ops: [ReelOp], _ document: ProjectDocument) -> [String: Any] {
884	        guard effectiveScope(document) != nil else { return [:] }
885	        let stamped = addressed(ops, document)
886	        var result: [String: Any] = [:]
887	        // Uniform by construction: `addressed` stamps ONE scope on every op it
888	        // stamps at all, so the first one found is the answer for all of them.
889	        if let nest = stamped.compactMap({ $0.scope }).first { result["scope"] = Int(nest) }
890	        let onDocument = stamped.filter { $0.scope == nil }.map { $0.name }
891	        if !onDocument.isEmpty { result["document_ops"] = onDocument }
892	        return result
893	    }
