692	    /// by `(to - start) × speed` so the frame under the edge does not move
693	    /// (spec §6).
694	    ///
695	    /// Clamped to the source bound — `to ≥ start - in/speed` snapped UP to
696	    /// a frame, `source_window.rs`'s `earliest_start_on_grid` (spec §11,
697	    /// D4-5) — and to leaving at least one frame of clip. Actions are
698	    /// deliberately left alone: clip-relative, rebasing would drop or bake
699	    /// whatever fell off the front, which is lossy and makes trimming back
700	    /// out not an identity. On a RAMPED clip the actions keep their clip
701	    /// time too, so `in` advances by round(S(Δ)) moving in and at the base
702	    /// rate moving out, and the edge is held where the clip still fits its
703	    /// media (`front_edge_held`, spec §11 SR-18 (c)): what plays after the
704	    /// new start can change. `text.words` are the opposite, on purpose
705	    /// (spec §11, W-3): a word is anchored to speech that stays where it is
706	    /// on the timeline, so every word is rebased by the trim's delta and a
707	    /// word trimmed off the front keeps its (negative) time. A graphic's
708	    /// `in` advances by the same delta (road map 10's G-9), with no speed
709	    /// and no head bound, never below 0.
710	    ///
711	    /// Without `alone`, a linked partner whose start is this clip's is
712	    /// trimmed to the same `to` after it, by its own clamps; with it, the
713	    /// clip moves alone — the J-cut (spec §11 AU-93, `carried_edge`).
714	    pub fn trim_start(&self, clip: u32, to: Ticks, alone: bool) -> Result<Outcome, String> {
715	        self.carried_edge(
716	            clip,
717	            alone,
718	            |target, partner| partner.start == target.start,
719	            |project, id| project.trim_start_one(id, to),
720	        )
721	    }
